1
00:00:00,313 --> 00:00:09,350
Long drives go wrong in predictable ways: low sun in your eyes,
a storm at the wrong hour, or darkness arriving before you do.

2
00:00:09,350 --> 00:00:14,829
GlarePilot checks weather and sun glare where,
and when, you will actually be driving.

3
00:00:14,829 --> 00:00:21,406
It has two clearly separated modes: real
data planning, and manual demo scenarios.

4
00:00:21,406 --> 00:00:28,737
Real-data planning first. Preset: Amherst → Chicago, leaving 8:00 AM tomorrow.

5
00:00:28,737 --> 00:00:36,172
The route and the forecast are retrieved live, right now.
Each point is evaluated at its projected arrival time.

6
00:00:36,172 --> 00:00:40,555
The trip summary shows the drive time and the expected arrival.

7
00:00:40,555 --> 00:00:47,469
On the map, the route is colored by sun glare,
and animated icons mark forecast trouble spots.

8
00:00:47,469 --> 00:00:53,175
Traffic is not predicted. Instead, you state
a delay. Here we assume one extra hour.

9
00:00:53,175 --> 00:00:59,301
The delay card shows the new arrival, and every
later point is re-timed against the forecast.

10
00:00:59,301 --> 00:01:05,060
Advice cards turn the conditions along the
route into short, specific recommendations.

11
00:01:05,060 --> 00:01:14,438
“When should I leave?” compares the same route departing
−3 h … +3 h, using a transparent exposure score.

12
00:01:14,438 --> 00:01:22,313
Lower bars mean less glare, wet road, wind and
after-dark exposure. The best option is marked.

13
00:01:22,313 --> 00:01:33,281
Next, manual demo scenarios. They use hand-set synthetic weather
on a recorded real route, with no weather or route API calls.

14
00:01:33,281 --> 00:01:43,452
“Delay shifts the weather” (synthetic): an assumed
75-minute delay pushes the arrival into rain and darkness.

15
00:01:43,452 --> 00:01:53,704
“Heavy rain + crosswind” (synthetic): rain and wind icons
appear where the hand-set squall crosses the route.

16
00:01:53,704 --> 00:02:05,048
“Snow, fog and night” (synthetic): hand-set snow over the
Berkshires, freezing fog near Worcester, all after dark.

17
00:02:05,048 --> 00:02:11,327
“Westbound sunset glare” (synthetic). Now we switch
from the 2D overview to the 3D chase preview.

18
00:02:11,327 --> 00:02:17,378
The chase preview is a simulated playback of the planned route. It is not GPS.

19
00:02:17,378 --> 00:02:22,664
Scrolling the wheel previews the road ahead.
Return jumps back to the playback position.

20
00:02:22,664 --> 00:02:30,555
Play runs a simulated drive along the planned route.

21
00:02:30,876 --> 00:02:39,977
Free, keyless data: Open-Meteo forecasts ·
Valhalla/OSRM routing · SunCalc sun position.

22
00:02:39,977 --> 00:02:49,874
A planning aid, not a safety system. Manual demos use
hand-set data. Built with Kiro for Hackachusetts 2026.
